  "featured_image_url": "https://thedrakelawfirm.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Common-Law-Marriage-in-Colorado.png",
  "content": {
    "rendered": "<p>A lot of people assume that if you live with someone long enough, you&#8217;re automatically <a href=\"https://thedrakelawfirm.com/common-law-marriage-attorney-lakewood-colorado/\">common law</a> married. That&#8217;s not how it works, and it&#8217;s one of the misconceptions I run into most often in my practice.</p>\n<p>Whether a common law marriage actually exists comes down to the specific facts of the relationship, not a calendar. Here&#8217;s what you should know.</p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-size: 1.7em;\">1. Living Together Isn&#8217;t Enough on Its Own</strong></p>\n<p>You can live with someone for years and still not be common law married. Colorado doesn&#8217;t have a magic number of years that triggers it. Courts look at the whole picture of the relationship, not a timeline.</p>\n<p><strong style=\"font-size: 1.7em;\">2. It Comes Down to Intent</strong></p>\n<p>The real question is whether <a href=\"https://thedrakelawfirm.com/moving-partner-avoid-common-law/\">both people intended to be married</a>. Courts look at the circumstances to figure out whether the couple actually agreed, in some way, that they were married to each other. Every relationship looks different, so there&#8217;s no single factor that settles it.</p>\n<h2><strong>3. Courts Look at a Range of Evidence</strong></h2>\n<p>When this question comes up, courts can weigh all kinds of things: how the couple presented themselves to others, how they handled money together, how they split responsibilities, and more. There&#8217;s no one document or action that proves or disproves a common law marriage on its own. Every case gets looked at on its own facts.</p>\n<h2><strong>4. If It&#8217;s Real, It Comes with Real Legal Consequences</strong></h2>\n<p>If a court finds that a valid common law marriage exists, both people generally have the same rights and obligations as anyone who had a formal wedding. That can affect property division, maintenance, inheritance, and other family law issues. Knowing where you stand matters before you make any big decisions.</p>\n<h3><strong>Know Where You Stand</strong></h3>\n<p>Common law marriage questions get complicated fast, and they play out differently for every couple. If you&#8217;re not sure how this applies to your situation, talk to a family law attorney who can walk through the facts with you.</p>\n<h3><strong>Have Questions About Common Law Marriage?</strong></h3>\n<h3><strong>Contact The Drake Law Firm</strong></h3>\n<p>If you&#8217;re dealing with a common law marriage question or any other family law issue we can help you understand your options. We handle divorce, property division, child custody, common law marriage, and more across Colorado. Reach out to schedule a consultation.</p>\n"
  },
